{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SuYErvjfYyTJk6MumfHidS","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have enough to form my findings. Let me compile them:\n\n## Architectural Friction Report: Almanac Bash Codebase\n\n**3 High-Impact Findings (ranked by impact):**\n\n---\n\n### 1. **harden-core.sh vs converge-core.sh: Parallel Duplication Without Abstraction** (Highest Impact)\n\n**File(s):** `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/harden-core.sh` (2039 lines) and `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/converge-core.sh` (995 lines)\n\n**Friction:** Both files define parallel role functions (`almanac_harden_role` vs `almanac_converge_role`) that differ only in role enum sets (harden: `reviewer`/`conductor`/`fixer`; converge: `agent`/`overseer`). Both pass through to identical `almanac_loop_role_config` (shared layer), yet the entry point is duplicated. The role-field resolution pattern appears twice. Deletion test: If you deleted `converge_role`, converge's role config would still resolve via `almanac_loop_role_config` — complexity doesn't concentrate, it just moves to the caller.\n\n**Seam:** The loop-adapter pattern (lib/loops.sh, lib/agent.sh) handles discovery and dispatch cleanly for providers and loop runners, yet role resolution is still split into loop-specific adapters in each core file.\n\n**Deepening sketch:** Extract role enums and defaults into loop adapters (lib/loops/harden.sh, lib/loops/converge.sh) as a `role_enum` contract verb, alongside `launch` and `control`. Collapse the two role functions to a single `almanac_loop_role` that queries the adapter. Move the duplicated role-field logic into a shared helper.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **lib/run.sh: God Module Owning 5 Concerns (registry + record + control + worker health + hub views)**\n\n**File:** `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/run.sh` (1042 lines)\n\n**Friction:** The module owns five unrelated concerns packed in consecutive function groups:\n- **Registry & record** (lines 41–133): TSV record I/O, field accessors\n- **Control** (lines 765–831): signal files, stop/steer directives\n- **Worker health** (lines 454–563): worker status parsing, file age, health scoring\n- **Hub views** (lines 605–739): rendering the hub overview and stats frames\n\nThese functions have no internal cohesion (record setters don't call worker health; control doesn't use TSV record). If you want to refactor hub rendering into a separate UI layer, you'd need to untangle it from control logic; if you want to move worker health to a diagnostics module, the same friction applies.\n\n**Deletion test:** Deleting sections would require re-implementing each concern's public seam independently — no complexity concentration, meaning each is a candidate for extraction.\n\n**Deepening sketch:** Split into: **lib/run-registry.sh** (record I/O + access), **lib/run-control.sh** (signal files, stop/steer), **lib/worker-health.sh** (worker diagnostics), and keep **lib/run.sh** as the thin launcher/status coordinator that imports the three. Hub rendering could migrate to lib/ui.sh as `almanac_ui_hub_render`.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **lib/loop-launcher.sh: Case-Statement Branching on Loop Type (not Loop-Adapter Dispatch)**\n\n**File:** `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/loop-launcher.sh` (408 lines, lines 399–408)\n\n**Friction:** The launcher's dispatcher is a hardcoded case statement (`ralph` → `_almanac_launch_ralph`, etc.) rather than using the loop-adapter dispatch seam that lib/loops.sh provides. This means adding a new loop requires editing the launcher; it does not auto-discover like provider adapters. The three launcher functions (`_almanac_launch_ralph`, `_almanac_launch_harden`, `_almanac_launch_converge`) are 122+ lines each and contain loop-specific config logic (ralph's `--prd` / `--mode`, harden's `--target` / `--lenses`, converge's `--goal` / `--exec`), leaking loop knowledge into a supposedly generic launcher.\n\n**Deletion test:** Deleting the case statement and moving each branch into lib/loops/{ralph,harden,converge}.sh as a `launch_config` contract verb would leave the launcher pure — complexity concentrates into adapters where it belongs.\n\n**Seam:** lib/loops.sh already has `almanac_loop_adapter_call` for dispatch; it's just not used. hub.sh also hardcodes loop-type checks (lines 92, 184, 193, 201, 223) when listing ralph/harden/converge.\n\n**Deepening sketch:** Add a `launch_config` contract to loop adapters. Collapse `_almanac_launch_*` into a single `almanac_loop_launch` that calls `almanac_loop_adapter_call \"$type\" launch_config [args]` and a shared validator. For hub.sh discovery, store the loop-type list in a loop adapter query (e.g., `almanac_loop_adapter_list`) rather than hardcoding it.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **cmd/hub.sh: Loop-Specific Resume/Clone Config Logic (Minor Seam Leak)**\n\n**File:** `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/cmd/hub.sh` (lines 183–213)\n\n**Friction:** `almanac_hub_resume_or_clone` contains a case statement that reconstructs config from the run status blob based on the loop type (lines 183–213). The status keys are loop-specific (`target`/`lenses` for harden, `goal`/`prompt`/`oversee_every` for converge, `iterations`/`oversee` for ralph), yet the hub doesn't own this schema — the loop runners do. Adding a loop config field requires editing both the loop runner and hub.sh.\n\n**Deepening sketch:** Move the reverse-mapping logic into loop adapters as a `status_to_config` contract verb that translates a status dict back into launcher options. Hub becomes a thin orchestrator.\n\n---\n\n### 5. **lib/feedback.sh & lib/worker.sh: Coherence (Positive)**\n\n**Status:** These modules are well-focused and show no shallow seams. Feedback owns only formatted output; worker owns only file-based worker coordination. They are candidates for deepening *other* modules, not themselves.\n\n---\n\n**Bottom line:** The three loops (ralph, harden, converge) follow a clean adapter pattern at the *runner* seam (lib/loops.sh + lib/loops/{ralph,harden,converge}.sh) but bypass it at the *launcher* and *role* seams. Unifying dispatch and extracting loop-specific config into adapters would make adding a fourth loop zero-friction."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a891a69f-75d4-4053-b662-17c3d63f7902","uuid":"10c54d9b-b013-494f-94b5-fcbfbf1f19e2","timestamp":"2026-05-26T14:38:09.941Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are exploring the Almanac codebase looking for architectural friction — shallow modules, leaky seams, and deepening opportunities. This is a Bash project for AI loop orchestration. CONTEXT.md vocabulary: Loop (ralph/harden/converge), Provider (claude/codex), Slug, Run, Worker, Launcher, Hub, Overseer, role/lens, provider adapter, loop adapter.\n\nKey files to evaluate (sizes already known):\n- lib/harden-core.sh — 2039 lines (largest)\n- lib/run.sh — 1042 lines (registry + record + control + worker health + hub views)\n- lib/converge-core.sh — 995 lines\n- lib/loop-launcher.sh — 408 lines\n- cmd/hub.sh — 390 lines\n- lib/agent.sh — 278 lines\n- lib/worker.sh — 189 lines\n- lib/loops.sh — 111 lines\n- lib/loops/{ralph,harden,converge}.sh — small adapters\n- lib/providers/{claude,codex}.sh — provider adapters\n- lib/feedback.sh — 129 lines\n- lib/role.sh — 93 lines\n\nI'm hunting for:\n1. **Shallow modules** — interface nearly as complex as the implementation (deletion test: would deleting it concentrate complexity? If \"no, complexity just moves\" → shallow).\n2. **God modules** that should be split — harden-core.sh at 2039 lines almost certainly mixes too many concerns.\n3. **Leaky seams** — where loop-specific knowledge appears in supposedly generic code (lib/run.sh, lib/loops.sh, cmd/hub.sh).\n4. **Parallel-but-not-quite** pairs — harden-core vs converge-core, same loop pattern duplicated rather than abstracted.\n5. **