nder (identity, live status — upgraded to `stale` when a running pid is gone — round/summary, start/finish); `almanac_loop_run_watch` renders it once off a TTY (the scripts-safe path) and redraws it on a `follow` TTY until the run reaches a terminal status. On a TTY, `cmd/hub.sh`'s `almanac_hub_menu` opens a selection menu whose top entry is always **+ New run**, followed by each running loop (each row carries `[id]`) and `quit`; selecting a running loop opens a watch/stop/steer/back action menu over it. The menu opens even with an empty registry (so New run is reachable with no prior runs), and **degrades gracefully without gum**: with gum it is a styled `gum choose`/`gum input`/`gum confirm`, and without gum it renders as a plain numbered menu read over stdin — both built on the shared `almanac_loop_ui_choose`/`almanac_loop_ui_input`/`almanac_loop_ui_confirm` seam, so the same menus work on any TTY. Off a TTY (piped/captured) the hub still renders the read-only overview once and exits. The **new-run** flow is itself built on a pure composer + non-interactive seam, mirroring the per-run actions: `almanac_loop_new_run_argv <type> [key=value…]` composes the launcher argv (one token per line, leading with the `ralph`/`harden` subcommand) — ralph config maps to ralph.sh flags (`--prd`/`--mode`/`--provider`/`--model`/`--effort`/`--iterations`/`--no-oversee`), harden launches the convergence loop (`harden <target> --loop [--rounds N]`); `almanac_loop_new_run_env <type> [key=value…]` composes the `KEY=VALUE` env a run needs beyond its argv (ralph emits none — all flags; harden's reviewer config rides on `HARDEN_LENSES`/`HARDEN_PROVIDER`/`HARDEN_MODEL`/`HARDEN_EFFORT`). Both return 1 for an unknown type and 2 for a missing required field (ralph: prd; harden: target), so a malformed run is rejected rather than launched. `cmd/hub.sh`'s `almanac_hub_launch_new` either prints the resolved `ENV… almanac <argv>` command (dry-run, the scripts-safe preview the gum confirm reuses) or exports the env and execs the launcher, handing over the terminal. The interactive New-run flow (`almanac_hub_new_run`) picks the loop type, then hands off to the shared **almanac launcher** (`almanac_loop_launch`, see the CLI section) — no run-config logic lives in the hub any more; the same launcher backs `almanac ralph` / `almanac harden`, so the New-run UX is identical to running those directly. (The non-interactive `--new` composer seam below stays for scripts/tests.) The seam is also drivable directly: `almanac hub --new <ralph|harden> [config…] [--dry-run]`. `tests/test-hub.sh` pins the non-interactive seams (`--steer` writes the steer file with the directive, `--stop` writes the stop file, `--watch` renders the run detail once off a TTY, `--new … --dry-run` composes the ralph/harden launch and rejects a run missing its required config).\n87:The **gum-or-plain UI seam** every consumer's dashboard composes from lives in its own module, **`lib/ui.sh`** — extracted from the engine (loop-engine-split slice 02) so each consumer (`lib/loop-launcher.sh`, `lib/harden-core.sh`, `cmd/hub.sh`, and `lib/run.sh` for the hub read views) sources it **directly** rather than borrowing it transitively; `lib/ui.sh` is self-contained (no dependency on `lib/core.sh`) and is its own test surface (`tests/test-ui.sh`). `almanac_loop_ui_has_gum` is the single gate for `gum` styling — true only when `gum` is installed, stdout is a TTY, and `ALMANAC_NO_GUM` is unset — so styled output is opt-out and never appears in piped/captured streams; `almanac_loop_ui_render` styles a stdin block as a rounded panel when that gate is open and passes it through unchanged otherwise (graceful degradation); `almanac_loop_ui_status_glyph` is a pure state→glyph map; and `almanac_loop_ui_clear` clears the terminal between redraw-loop frames only when stdout is a TTY (a no-op off a terminal, so it never pollutes piped/captured output). It also owns the **interactive selection primitives** the hub menus are built on — `almanac_loop_ui_choose` (pick one of N options), `almanac_loop_ui_input` (free-text value with optional default), and `almanac_loop_ui_confirm` (yes/no) — each gum-styled (`gum choose`/`gum input`/`gum confirm`) when `almanac_loop_ui_has_gum` is open and degrading to a plain numbered menu / `read` prompt otherwise, so menu-driven flows work on any TTY without gum. The selection *logic* is split into pure, unit-tested helpers — `almanac_loop_ui_menu_render` (options → numbered list) and `almanac_loop_ui_menu_pick` (typed number → chosen option, rejecting blank/non-numeric/out-of-range) — with the choose/input/confirm wrappers adding only the gum-or-`read` I/O around them; the prompt goes to stderr so callers capture only the choice on stdout. `lib/worker.sh` also owns `almanac_loop_worker_watch`, which streams one worker's live `events.jsonl` so any consumer can watch a single reviewer/fixer — reading the worker's events-file path from its `status.tsv`, tailing it live (`tail -f`) on a TTY and printing its current contents once otherwise (so pipes and tests never block). **Worker health detection** (`lib/run.sh`) classifies a reviewer's progress so the dashboard can surface a stalled (log stopped advancing), idle (running but never produced an event), or looping (same event repeating) worker: `almanac_loop_worker_health` is a pure predicate over `(status, log-age, event-count, trailing-repeat, stall-threshold, loop-threshold) → running|stalled|idle|looping|done|failed` (table-tested with no files/clock), fed by the gather wrapper `almanac_loop_worker_health_of` (with `almanac_loop_file_age_secs`, `almanac_loop_count_lines`, and `almanac_loop_trailing_repeat` measuring the worker's `events.jsonl`).\n89:The **provider-adapter seam** every consumer's provider knowledge routes through lives in **`lib/agent.sh`** + **`lib/providers/<name>.sh`** — extracted from the engine (loop-engine-split slice 03). Each backend is a drop-in adapter file (`lib/providers/codex.sh`, `lib/providers/claude.sh`) implementing one fixed contract — `available`, `active_env`, `models`, `efforts`, `display`, `argv`, `filter` — and adapters are **auto-discovered**: the provider list IS the files present (`almanac_provider_list`), so there is no central provider list to keep in sync. The seam is **deep on invocation**: the adapter's `argv` yields the full exec tokens — including the sandbox→permission mapping (claude's `--permission-mode`) and `--json`-or-not by shape — into `_ALMANAC_AGENT_ARGV`, and its `filter` yields the jq event-stream program, so **no `codex …`/`claude …` literal lives outside an adapter**. `lib/agent.sh` owns discovery, name-normalising dispatch (`almanac_provider_call <name> <verb>` — the one place that turns a provider name into a function call, so no caller ever branches on provider name), and the single central remainder: the **default-selection policy** (`almanac_provider_default` — the active-env provider if installed, else the first installed in preference order claude → codex), which unifies ralph's runtime detection (`once.sh`/`afk.sh` `detect_provider`) with the launcher's provider menu so they agree. Self-contained like `lib/ui.sh` (no dependency on `lib/core.sh`), it is its own test surface (`tests/test-providers.sh`, exercising discovery, availability, active-env, menus, the codex/claude `argv` shapes, the sandbox→permission mapping, and default-selection — all behind a fake `command -v`, no real model calls). Consumers source it **directly**: the agent run shapes in `lib/agent.sh` (which dispatch argv + filter through the seam and own only execution mechanics; consumed by `lib/worker.sh`'s worker fan-out, `lib/harden-core.sh`, and ralph's `once.sh`/`afk.sh`), `lib/loop-launcher.sh` (its provider menu / availability / list), and ralph's `once.sh`/`afk.sh` (detection / availability / display name).\n91:The **loop-adapter seam** mirrors the provider one for loops, and lives in **`lib/loops.sh`** + **`lib/loops/<name>.sh`** — extracted from the engine (loop-engine-split slice 08). Each loop is a drop-in adapter file (`lib/loops/ralph.sh`, `lib/loops/harden.sh`, `lib/loops/converge.sh`) and adapters are **auto-discovered**: the loop list IS the files present (`almanac_loop_adapter_list`), so there is no central loop list to keep in sync. `lib/loops.sh` owns discovery and name-normalising dispatch (`almanac_loop_adapter_call <name> <verb>` — the one place a loop type becomes a function call, so no caller branches on loop type). Adapters can declare **launch** — `exec_argv` populates `_ALMANAC_LOOP_ARGV` with the runner's exec tokens (ralph's `mode`→`once.sh`/`afk.sh` under the ralph-loop skill; harden's `almanac harden <target> --loop [--rounds N]`), so the **runner path lives in the adapter, not the launcher**. Adapters also share a **control** contract — the between-round dot-file basename for `stop|steer`. Every loop today uses the same `.${name}-${kind}` convention (`.ralph-stop`/`.ralph-steer`, `.harden-stop`/`.harden-steer`, `.converge-stop`/`.converge-steer`), so the pattern lives once in `lib/loops.sh` as `almanac_loop_default_signal_file` and adapters need not declare `signal_file` at all unless their convention diverges. The public resolver `almanac_loop_signal_file` returns the adapter override if defined else the default — so adding a standard new loop costs zero signal_file code, and three identical case-statements no longer have to stay in sync across adapters. `lib/run.sh` exposes `almanac_loop_run_control_file <type> <base> <kind>` as the absolute-path variant (BASE joined with the resolved basename), so harden-core and converge-core no longer reimplement their own `_control_file` wrapper each. The **launcher** (`lib/loop-launcher.sh`) consumes launch — `_almanac_launch_ralph`/`_almanac_launch_harden` exec their runner via `almanac_loop_adapter_call <type> exec_argv …` rather than a hard-coded path — and the **hub** consumes control through `almanac_loop_run_signal_file` (above). Self-contained like `lib/agent.sh` (no dependency on `lib/core.sh` — only printf/tr/basename/source), it is its own test surface (`tests/test-loops.sh`: discovery, dispatch rejecting an unimplemented verb, the `signal_file` control mapping, and the `exec_argv` launch composition for loops against a fake `$ALMANAC_HOME`). Adding a loop is one new file under `lib/loops/`; nothing central changes.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
